-- title:
--   The Type I sum is bounded by its odd-divisor envelope
-- statement:
--   Let $x\ge1$, $U\ge0$, and $V\ge1$, with $UV\le x/4$ and $x\le UV^2$. The finite Type I sum is bounded by the sum of the explicit per-divisor envelope $E_x(\alpha,d)$ over positive odd integers $d\le\lfloor UV\rfloor$:
--   $$|T_I(x,\alpha,U,V)|\le\sum_{\substack{1\le d\le\lfloor UV\rfloor\\d\text{ odd}}}E_x(\alpha,d).$$
--   This reduces the exponential sum to a nonnegative scalar sum while preserving the cutoff support.
-- source:
--   Terence Tao, Every odd number greater than 1 is the sum of at most five primes, https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6656, Sections 5 and 6. Original Lean source: Theorems/Thm_TaoFivePrimes_vaughan_typeI_ordinary_final_assembly.lean, lines 97-133.

